This case study combines three layers with different terms, plus a third-party
rights statement that is not optional. Please respect all four.

1. VOCABULARY, MAPPINGS AND DOCUMENTATION
   The ontology (ontology/*.ttl), the crosswalk mappings (crosswalk/*.ttl), the
   SHACL shapes (shapes/*.ttl), the generated coverage outputs
   (pipeline/coverage-report.md, pipeline/coverage-metrics.json) and the
   documentation (README.md, BUILD_REPORT.md, CHANGELOG.md, CONTRIBUTING.md,
   shapes/README.md) are licensed under CC BY 4.0:
     https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/
   Attribute "The Tesseract Academy: Robot safety and security crosswalk
   (RSSC)".

2. PIPELINE SCRIPTS AND DEMO
   The scripts under pipeline/ (including the self-test fixtures under
   pipeline/selftest/) and the offline browser demo/index.html are licensed
   under the MIT Licence (see below).

3. THIRD-PARTY STANDARDS: ISO AND IEC
   No normative text from any ISO or IEC standard is reproduced in this case
   study. What is recorded is the public surface of those documents: standard
   designations, part numbers, editions, publication dates, clause identifiers,
   clause titles, annex letters, annex normative status, scopes and
   normative-reference lists, all read from freely published front matter,
   contents pages, previews and catalogue entries, and cited as facts.

   Definitions attached to concepts in this vocabulary are this project's own
   paraphrase, written so that a reader without the standards can use the
   vocabulary, and are labelled as paraphrase wherever they restate a paywalled
   definition.

   ISO and IEC standards remain the copyright of ISO and IEC respectively and
   must be purchased from the issuing body or its national distributors. Nothing
   in this licence grants any right in them. The CC BY 4.0 licence in section 1
   applies to this project's own vocabulary, mappings and prose, and to nothing
   else.

4. INDEPENDENCE
   This is independent, self-initiated open research. It is not endorsed by,
   affiliated with or approved by ISO, IEC, CEN, CENELEC, BSI, ISA, the ISA
   Security Compliance Institute, IEEE, MITRE, KAN or any other body named
   within it. It is not a conformity assessment and confers no certification.

